     1	# Chapter 39 — Aimed
     2	
     3	Vastin had been given three words in his life for the thing that he had served, and he set them out in their order, since he had never been able to think about a matter until it was set out.
     4	
     5	He did it on a box, by a bay of bundles, in the afternoon of the two hundred and ninety-third day. Nobody wanted him. The lamp had come back to the long table, and the boy knelt beside it, a hand to the left, as he had knelt on every other afternoon. The small pale woman was making a second copy. The man in the dusty coat sat in his chair with his eyes shut. It was a room with one office in it, and Vastin had not once that day felt his boot begin to move.
     6	
     7	The first word was *imperfect*.
     8	
     9	He had held that one for very nearly the whole of his service, and he had held it comfortably. A scale has an error. A careful officer learns the error, and allows for it, and does not throw away the scale. He had sat through eleven fields and nine thousand readings on that footing. Where a plate gave a wrong answer he had called it a wrong answer, and had written so, and had been thought rather too scrupulous by people who were not obliged to sign.
    10	
    11	The second had been given him by a file.
    12	
    13	He had read it by the candles of four inns, a long while ago, and had carried it up a bluff to a house above Ostrand; and there five young people had shown him what the office that he served did with honest readings. He had said one sentence afterward, to a boy, in a room that was not his own. He had it by heart. He had never been sure that the boy did.
    14	
    15	*I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be.*
    16	
    17	That had cost him a seal, left in a tray on a morning of rain, and three hundred miles on a hedge-stick. He had thought it the end of the matter. A man finds that the scale was made to read false; he lays down his office; he goes to dig up the one buried thing that he has the knowledge to find. He had supposed, on the road, that the thing would turn out to be a fraud. He had been ready for a fraud. He had dealt with a great many.
    18	
    19	It was not a fraud. A fraud wants something. It leans the scale so that a house may keep its place, or a seat its revenue, and when the leaning is found there is at least a person behind it who has got richer.
    20	
    21	He had heard a leaf of skin read out that morning, once, by a woman who would not say it twice. He did not say it over. He had been asked not to, and he was a man who kept to what he was asked; he had learned that lately, and at some cost.
    22	
    23	But he could not help knowing what he had heard. A scale that reads false is still a scale, and whoever stands on it is at least weighed. This was another matter.
    24	
    25	That was not *imperfect*, and it was not *designed*. He turned over what it was, on a box, with his hands on an ash stick; and the word came up of its own accord, as a third figure comes when two have been written.
    26	
    27	*Aimed.*
    28	
    29	He looked at that for some time.
    30	
    31	He had put his hand to the foot of a great many sheets. He went over them now, since he owed it. Before the fields, and before the readings, he had been a junior officer of a district seat, and it had been his work on certain mornings to stand beside a desk while a child of fourteen was told what it was to be. A clerk wrote. He signed. It had been given to him as a duty of no weight, such as is given to the young, and he had thought it one.
    32	
    33	He remembered very few of them. That was a finding in itself. He remembered a boy who had laughed aloud, and been hushed. He remembered a girl in a red cap, who had looked up afterward, as they all did, to find a face; and he had been reading the sheet.
    34	
    35	The sentence rose in him then.
    36	
    37	It was the one that he had begun that morning, to a bay of bundles, six strides from a desk. He knew its first half as he knew his own name. It came up exactly as a hand had once come up toward a lamp-ring, half-way there before he had seen it start; and he gave it the look that he had given the hand.
    38	
    39	It was a draft. A small grey man had told him at a spring what he was when he drafted, and a small pale woman had laid a bare leaf on a desk and asked him to put nothing on it. He did not finish it. He did not let himself hear how it would have ended, even in the place behind his eyes where a man hears his own clauses before he speaks them.
    40	
    41	It rose twice more before the light was trimmed. He put it down each time, and each time it was a little easier, like the leg.
    42	
    43	There was one thing that he permitted himself, since it was not a conclusion. It was only something that he had done.
    44	
    45	He looked along the tables at the boy.
    46	
    47	Three nights ago, in a stair, six people had come to see what that boy was; and he had stood upon a step with his stick, on the eleventh day of thirty, and put a wall in front of him. He had not known then what stood behind it. He had said so, on a pallet, when he could not hold a bowl. He knew a little more this afternoon. He would do it again, knowing; and that in a day which had taken the floor out from under forty years, it was the single act of his that he could look at without wishing to revise.
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	He asked for the stair a quarter of an hour before the bell.
    52	
    53	"I shall want the start," he said to the boy, who had come to see what he needed. "And I should prefer to take it by myself. I have had your hand under my heel, or at the other end of a strap, on every flight for three days. I am obliged for each of them, and I should like to learn whether I can manage one stair without."
    54	
    55	"If it goes, there's nobody below you."
    56	
    57	"If it goes, I shall sit down, which I shall be doing in any case."
    58	
    59	The eyes that looked back at him were tired. They were nineteen years old, and they had heard what he had heard, and they had gone back to holding a lamp.
    60	
    61	"I'll be a flight behind," said the boy. "I'll not come nearer unless you call."
    62	
    63	It was a hundred and ten strides to the cross-way, on the stick and the uprights. He put the strap of the tarred case over his head, so that it hung at his back. Then he turned about at the foot of the first flight, and let himself down, and began.
    64	
    65	It was dark from the second tread. He had no need of light; he had learned those steps in three days as a blind man learns a room. He set his two hands on the stone behind him, and thrust with the sound leg, and came up by one. The bound leg went before him, stiff, like a thing that he had been asked to mind for somebody else.
    66	
    67	A man who goes up a stair backward looks at where he has been.
    68	
    69	There was nothing to see but the dark at the foot, and a little grey in it where a lamp burned a long way off down a gallery; but he faced it, tread after tread, and it grew smaller.
    70	
    71	He thought about the walls.
    72	
    73	He had been a wall. He did not say that with any pity for himself; it was the plainest account of Iron Wall, and of his office, and of what a seat had used him for. A crowd came to a gate, or a hearing drew too many, and he was sent for. He struck his stick upon the ground, and whatever stood on the far side of the line stayed there. He had done it eleven times for a seat, and twice since for no seat at all. He had believed, each of the eleven times, that the thing behind him was the law, and that it was imperfect, and that it was a great deal better than what would come through if he lifted his heel.
    74	
    75	He would have to go over the eleven, one at a time, when he had a table and a month. It was the kind of work that he was good at.
    76	
    77	Nine. The first landing. He rested with his hands open on his thighs.
    78	
    79	What he could not get past was smaller than the eleven, and it had no file.
    80	
    81	He had met the boy across a desk, with a plate between them, on a bluff. He had read him for three days, with every instrument that the seat owned, and had written that the instruments could not reach him. He had declined to read him again. He had told the boy why, on a bank, at dusk: the sound reason, and the poor one beside it.
    82	
    83	He had thought, through all of that, that he was dealing with an error. A plate had failed upon one child, as a scale fails upon a weight that is out of its range. It had been his whole understanding of the case, and he had built a resignation and a road upon it.
    84	
    85	He stopped there, on the dark stone, with one hand lifted to the next tread. He could feel that the thought had a second half; and the second half was the sentence again, come round by another door. He saw it for what it was. *Draft nothing. Just look.* He put the hand down upon the stone, which was cold and dry and laid without mortar, and looked at that instead until the thing had gone by.
    86	
    87	He went on up.
    88	
    89	There was a matter that he could settle, and he gave the second flight to it. They would sit in a yard tonight. The big young man had said so at noon: there would be a fire by the stall door, since one of them could not climb a ladder; and he knew who the one was. He would be at that fire. The leaf would be read there by those who had not heard it. And the boy would be across the flames from him, with all of it to carry.
    90	
    91	He owed that boy a sentence.
    92	
