     1	# Chapter 43 — Here
     2	
     3	There were sheep on the high moor, and that was the first thing which told Cael that the country had changed while they were under a town.
     4	
     5	He saw them at the third mile on the two hundred and ninety-sixth morning: a scatter of grey backs on the swell ahead, with their heads down in the bents and the east wind laying their wool over. There had been none on that ground sixteen days ago. A flock is not turned out where eight things lie up round a hole in the hill.
     6	
     7	At the fourth mile the ruts began to draw in toward a black place in the rock, and a spare grey person stood beside them with a crook.
     8	
     9	It was the factor. There was the leather apron, and the slate on its cord, and the pair of pale eyes that had gone along six people and a healer at a gate and counted them. They counted now. They went from a quiet man, who had come up level with the cobs, to four with loads; and they rested last upon the tail-board, where a pair of raw hands lay on an ash stick.
    10	
    11	"Five," said the factor. "And the one that walked behind. He's not behind today."
    12	
    13	"He's paid for where he's sitting," said Brom.
    14	
    15	"I'd not doubt it. He paid my woman a copper to be told where the straw was." The crook went out toward the moor. "Those are mine. I've not had a ewe on this top since the first frost, and I'd three hundred lambed in the yard for want of it. I've had thirty-one carts up that cut in a fortnight, where I told you thirty. The salting-shed's full to the door."
    16	
    17	Nobody answered, since it was a road. But they were at the lip of a hold's own ground, with the hold's keeper speaking, and Cael judged that the rule would bear a low voice there, as it had borne one in that yard.
    18	
    19	"We're glad of it," he said.
    20	
    21	"I didn't come up onto this top to make you glad. I've a thing to put to you, and I'll put it once, since I was told beforehand what I'd hear." The pale eyes had come to Brom, who had the purse. "That seep'll have its glass back at the first frost. Another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. I said so on that bank with your wet one standing by. I'd as soon not sit in my yard and wait to learn which."
    22	
    23	"What's the offer?" said Brom.
    24	
    25	"Twenty, at each first frost, on the same bar, to the five that walk it before I let a wheel on. The long house's fire for the week that it takes, and the pot."
    26	
