    49	---
    50	
    51	The dray went into the cut first, with its wheels in the water.
    52	
    53	There was no glass. A fortnight and more of thaw had come up that hill since a stick cracked it, and the seep ran down the middle of the road now as a seep ought, a stride wide and a finger deep, talking over the stones. The lean man had his near cob by the cheek-strap. He walked it down on the wet with its head held, and the other followed, as beasts had been walked down that place all through a cold term by people who had the nerve.
    54	
    55	The six went after on the dry edge, in file.
    56	
    57	Cael had gone up this strip of gravel with a twist of tow in his fist and his mouth as dry as the tow. He had come down it afterward at a walk, a little bent, with eight things pouring over a bank behind him. He knew every yard of it. The black rock stood at the head, with the water coming out under it in a skin; the scree hung on his right hand like a steep roof, where a girl had lain on plates; and on his left the bank was no higher than his belt, with three worn places in its upper half.
    58	
    59	It was the same cut. It was not the place that he had been afraid in, and he could not at first have said what was different. Then he had it. The cold was gone out of it. On that day the air had come up to meet him like the breath of a cellar, and now it was only the shade of a hill.
    60	
    61	Lira went down it with her face turned up to the scree. She had lain on those plates with a knife, on a day when the left arm could do no more than hold. He saw her mark the place, and shut that hand once at her side, and look away. Karis did not look at the banks at all. She was watching the water go under the dray's wheels, with her lips moving, like a person who has written six findings about a thing and suspects that there is a seventh.
    62	
    63	Behind him the stick's sound had stopped.
    64	
    65	Vastin had got down at the head, as he had undertaken to do for every slope. He was coming down the dry edge last of all, a tread at a time, with the bound leg swung out stiff; and forty strides from the top he was standing still.
    66	
