    79	He did not lift it far. The ash came up off the gravel by the breadth of a hand, and was held there, level, in his right fist; and the whole of him came over onto the bound leg, as a load is let down onto a prop that nobody is yet sure of. Cael saw the slat take it. He saw the grey mouth shut hard. It was one breath, and the half of another.
    80	
    81	The stick came down.
    82	
    83	The knee had held. It was not mended; anybody could have seen that, by the sweat that had sprung on the old forehead in that cold shade. But it had borne a man once, with nothing beside it, on the very ground where eight hounds had been stopped by it and where four holes had been torn in it afterward. He had not been told to try it. He had not asked leave of anybody; and Cael noticed that for once he did not turn to see who would enter the thing.
    84	
    85	Brom had turned on his stone, below. He had put out one flat hand, without knowing that he did it, as a man steadies the foot of a ladder that somebody else is on; and when the ash came down he let the hand fall.
    86	
    87	"Held," he said, low.
    88	
    89	The old man looked from the big man to the stones. He had said one word in that cut, and had been given one. Seventeen days ago, on the same ground, the loudest thing on the whole moor had been his.
    90	
    91	"Two words," Vastin said. "Both of them ours."
    92	
    93	"Noted," Cael said.
    94	
    95	He had given that word to a captain three times, at a hearth and on a landing. He had never before given it to this man. It was a clerk's word, and he had kept it from him as a person keeps his purse from somebody who once had the counting of it.
    96	
    97	It did not feel like a clerk's word on that road. It felt like a stroke made at the foot of a slip.
    98	
    99	Lira's two fingers had gone up, below, where the dray's tail was going out into the light. They went on down.
