     1	# Chapter 44 — A Wall's Count
     2	
     3	Teague counted them off the scow, from the window on the rise, as he counted anything that came over that river.
     4	
     5	There were six, and there was no mule; and he marked the mule's absence first. A crew that went west with a beast and came home without one had sold it, or eaten it, or lent it; and that crew did not sell what belonged to a healer. So she was off on her own road, with the beast under her, and they had carried three boxes on their backs for as long as she had been gone.
     6	
     7	They carried them poorly: the big one had his on the right shoulder, which was the bound one, and neither of his arms came up above the elbow when he shifted it. The small pale one walked with her left hand laid flat against her coat. The girl had both her arms, which was new since the thaw; she used the left as a person uses a tool that has just come back from the smith. The boy with the book was sound, and came last but one, as he always had.
     8	
     9	Last of all a stick came off the planks, and a stiff leg after it, and then the old man.
    10	
    11	Teague looked at him longer than at the rest.
    12	
    13	He had been home from the shoulder hold for a week. He had taken that salt up himself, behind the first carts, when he might have sent it with the bow and saved his own boots; the bow had said so, and had been right. But a cut that had eaten three carts in one cold term had been opened in an afternoon by the eleventh crew on Pike's cloth, and Teague had wished to see the ground.
    14	
    15	There had been little to see, since it was a wet road between two banks. He had stood in it for a quarter of an hour, and then he had gone up to the gate, where a grey factor with a crook was counting sacks; and he had asked what was done there, and by whom.
    16	
    17	He had been told about a rock, and a knife, and a boy who lit a thing no bigger than a barley grain and walked away from it. He had expected as much, since those five fought as he would have had his own fight, with nothing showing. Then the factor told him the rest.
    18	
    19	Eight had come down that road at a run, and had stopped in the air.
    20	
    21	He had made the factor say it twice. The old man with the stick had stepped out, and struck the ice once, and there had been a thing across the cut from one bank to the other, as high as the scree. Eight hounds broke on it. It stood for exactly as long as the man never lifted a foot; and when it went, he went down with it, like a tent with the pole drawn.
    22	
    23	Teague had never seen an Iron Wall. He had heard of them, as a man who works the holds hears of what is kept behind the walls of towns. They belonged to tribunals, and a person did not come by one in a meadow. Whoever carried such a thing up a border road on a stick had been somebody, a long way from here, and had left off being it; and Teague was not such a fool as to ask what.
    24	
    25	He had asked something else, and it was what a captain needed to know. Could the man move?
    26	
    27	He could not. That was the whole of it, and Teague had carried it home down a moor, turning it over.
    28	
    29	The Force went out along a line and was done. He had been paid for that for the better part of his life, and it had made him first on a sheet of sailcloth: he could put his palm to a thing that six men could not shift, and it shifted. It was a thing for roads: a block came down on a ledge, or a wheel went into a ditch, or a gate would not open; and he arrived, and it was settled, and the wrist kept the account.
    30	
    31	A wall did not arrive. It stayed. It could not come to the trouble; the trouble had to be brought to it, and whoever stood behind it had to be worth the bringing. He had seen that much from a factor's telling; the boy with the book had seen it from two feet away, and had gone and stood there.
    32	
    33	So a wall wanted a count. It was the first thing that such a tool asked of whoever meant to use it: how many are behind? A captain who did not know that figure before the thing went up would learn it afterward, by who was left outside.
    34	
    35	He had it counted before he was off the moor. There were their five, and the old man made six; there were his own three, and himself; and it made ten.
    36	
    37	The scow's bow touched. He watched six people go up the bank toward the lower inn, with a stick behind them, and saw that the stick was no longer a dozen strides back.
    38	
    39	---
    40	
    41	"It pays nothing," said the bow.
    42	
    43	He was on the settle, with the bad leg to the heat, running a thumb along a string that did not want it. He had said the same on a night of snow, and again when the ice went; he was a man who liked a thing said three times before he would leave it alone.
    44	
    45	"It pays nothing that I can show you," said Teague.
    46	
    47	"Then what's it for? We've the cloth. We've salt work waiting on four holds, and a weir at the mill that wants three stakes yet. I'm not a young man, and that's a week's walk to stand at the edge of a field and watch another crew go into it."
    48	
    49	"We'll not be watching."
    50	
    51	"We'll not be going in to any purpose, either. You said it yourself, at their hearth. In there I'm a man with a bad hip."
    52	
    53	Teague did not answer at once. He owed that man a true answer; the bow had walked behind him for a great many cold terms, and had never once asked to be told a comfortable thing.
    54	
    55	"I've been first on that sheet a long while," he said. "It says who brought the goods home. That's all it was ever for, and I've never asked more of it." He looked at the fire. "For the whole of a cold term the only thing in this country worth a man's knowing went on in a shed down that bank, behind a hurdle, with a guard on the door. I was first, and I was on the wrong side of the hurdle. I didn't care for it."
    56	
    57	"So it's pride."
    58	
    59	"It's that I mean to be at the front of the thing. I'll not dress it. A captain who tells his people that he's going for their sake, when he's going for his own, gets them killed for a lie." He turned his head. "You're not bound. I said my crew comes, and I said it to fifty people. I never said that every man of it must."
    60	
    61	The bow snorted, and let the string go.
    62	
    63	"I'd like to see you find the mill without me," he said. It was his whole answer, and Teague took it as he would have taken a thumb on a slip.
    64	
    65	The old woman had not looked up from her board, where she was cutting linen into lengths with a small knife.
    66	
    67	"There's a valley up the north water that breaks its legs every dry spell," she said. "It's done so without me before now. I'm coming for that wrist. I'll not be counted, and I'll not be told where to stand."
    68	
    69	"I'd not have counted you."
    70	
    71	"See that the others don't."
    72	
    73	The boy came with the cloth.
