editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:Karis had the second chair, with a board across her knees and her notebook open upon the board. She read the first line through in the time that it takes to lace a boot; she had every one of the twenty-three letters now, and a hand that had pressed like a mason's did not blur. Then she sat looking at what she had written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:57:"I can spell it," she said. "I can very nearly say it aloud, as a child says a word in a tongue that it has not been taught. It is a noun, and then a number, and the number I can read." Her pencil tapped. "The noun stands on the face as well. It is the first of the four things that he says were made."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:73:He did not read as she read. He set his pen at the foot of the first line and drew what was there, sign for sign, as a joiner copies a moulding, without once asking what it said; and wherever a run came down flat he made a small tick beneath it, in red, from a second pot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:75:"She'll take hers away, and it'll be right," he said, to the skin. "And in a month she'll want to know whether a stroke was the writer's or the knife's, and she'll be three hundred miles off. So there's to be one that's drawn and not read. That's mine. It goes with her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:81:That was all, and they went on, two pens at one leaf. There was a quick small hand that turned a dead tongue into words, and a slow square one that turned it into nothing and kept its shape. Cael held the flame along the skin for both, and moved it up a finger as each line was done. He did not need to be told. He had known for three days where the red eyes would go next, and he kept his own upon his knuckles while he knew it.
