editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:175:He had not looked at the boy. He had not done so since he poured a cup of water and set it on a table's corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:181:Vastin had asked for his quarter of an hour again, and had been given it. They heard him above them on the second flight when they came to the stair's foot with the boxes: two hands set down, and a breath, and a scrape. He was at the head when they reached it, sitting against the iron with the case in his arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:187:Quick flat feet came up behind them, with a brown light. The man drew the bar. He did not swing the iron at once. He stood with his hand on it, in the narrow place at the stair's head, with five people and three boxes pressed along the wall; and he held out a square of card-stock, rolled, with a turn of brown tape about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:191:"I do not know how to enter this week," she said. "I have been trying since the yard. I have a page for what the leaf says, and a page that is clean. Neither of them has a line for the man who gave me every join in fifty-two sheets, and would not hear what I thought they were. I shall have to write something at the head; and I should like it to be a thing that you would put your name to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:209:They had come up the only stair in sight, all twenty-seven steps of it, and it lay behind them in the dark. Cael watched her take that in, and then watched her take in the rest. There had been a row of signs on a sheet, with each one hung from the one before; and a man had said that he did not read such a thing, but was walked down it. She had fifty-two sheets of that in three boxes, and she was about to walk them east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:213:The iron went to behind them, and they heard the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:219:Cael watched him find it. The pen had set down *the same* on that leaf day after day, and tonight the same was not so. The tables below were bare. Three boxes stood upon his paving which would not come back in the morning, and there was no card for the morning in anybody's hand. His eyes went along the stones at the height of a boot, and found seven that stood and one that hung. Then they went back to the form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:221:He wrote slowly. *Withdrawn by its bearer*, said the long column; and the narrow one said *four*. He blotted both. Then, with the pen still lifted, he ruled a line beneath the whole of it, across both columns, as a man rules off an account that has come out even.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:227:The clerk had asked them one thing in eleven days, and he did not ask a second. But when the key had turned in the low door he stood a moment on its sill, with his book against his chest, and looked along the dock at an old man being got onto a handcart. His mouth opened a very little, as it had on the first morning. Then he shut it, and went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:237:The yard was nearly dark when they had the cart swept. Brom did it himself, with a birch broom from the stable, and stood the shafts against the wall as he had found them. Then he looked at the three boxes in their row, and at his own two arms, which hung.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:239:"I can carry mine, on the right, as I have," he said. "She's two, and so's he. That's the boxes. She's the flat and the hide." He nodded toward the mounting block. "And there's twelve miles of that road before the first hedge ends, and a hill after it, and I've nothing to put under him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:241:"I shall walk for as long as I am able," said Vastin. "I shall say when I am not. I undertake that much, and I do not undertake a yard more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:253:He looked at it with her. It was thinner than the right, still, all down the forearm, and its eight marks were pale; but it turned without a hitch, and when she shut the fist there was no tremor in it that he could see. Twenty-nine days ago it had come out of a sling, and could do no more than hold a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:*Two hundred and ninety-fourth day. Treswick; the loft, and one in the stall; the last night of both. A hundred and thirty. The back of the leaf: a list, nine lines, a thing and its number to each. Two copies, hers and the reader's, and his goes with her. The fourth line is the mark's; of its last six signs, three are sure. Fifty-two sheets counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote* withdrawn*. Vastin: fifteen of thirty, and the stair alone. Karis wrote with the sewn hand. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:261:He read that last over, and let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:265:She was four days off, and there was still no mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:9:So Lira went up the ladder in the eastern gallery, with a book as broad as a paving stone against her chest, and Brom stood at the foot with the ring in his left fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:19:She stood by the second chair, with her notebook against her chest. She had slept; he could see that she had, and that it had been done on purpose, as a person eats before a long road; and it had not taken the white out of her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:"I should like one of you here. I do not wish to read it to a chair, and a man who shuts his eyes." She drew a breath. "You have knelt at this for eight days, and been given a sore knee for it. I think that you are owed the end of the week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:29:There was a fourth in that end of the gallery. Vastin sat on Brom's box by the nearest bay, six strides from the desk, with the bound leg out along its plank and both hands on the ash; he could not have followed a ladder, and he was only where he had been set down, as he was every morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:33:He took the bundle in both hands; it was thicker than a loaf, and tied across both ways, and it left a clean square in the dust where it had lain. He set it upon the slope of his desk, under the good lamp, and stood looking at the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:37:It took him some time. The tape had gone brown and stiff, and it cracked under his nails like the skin of an onion; but the knot came, a turn at a time, and he laid the two lengths out to either side as flat as rulers. Under the tape there were two thin boards of beech, and he lifted the upper one away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:41:It was a single leaf, and it was not paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:43:It had the colour of old horn, and it was stiff, like horn, and a little curled at one corner; and where the lamp struck the curl, the light came through it, brown and clouded, as it comes through the pane of a stable lantern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:45:It had not been written with a pen; he saw that at once, from his side of the desk, with the head of it away from him. There was no ink that lay upon the surface. Every sign had been pressed into the skin with a point, as a thing is scored on a slate, and some dark stuff had been rubbed into the scoring afterward and had mostly gone. The lines stood in it like the tracks of a very small bird across a crust of snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:47:They were the floors' letters, and he could not read one of them; but he had held a lamp above that woman's column for eight days, and he knew a hooked upright when he saw it, and a gate, and a fork.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:51:It lay a little below the middle, where for the width of four or five letters the skin was paler, and thinner, and rough, as a board is rough where a plane has gone against the grain. Something had been taken out of that line with an edge. Whoever did it had been thorough; and had stopped, all the same, a hair too soon. At the left side of the pale place a single stroke was left standing. It was upright, and as long as a letter is tall, and it led to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:The thing behind Cael's eyes had come round to that leaf as it had come round to nothing since a plank of oak, and it leaned as a dog leans on a leash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:69:She stood at the slope of the desk, where he had stood, with her notebook open beside the skin and her pencil upright in her right hand. The left lay flat upon the oak, in its strip of linen. Cael saw her eyes go to the first sign, at the head, in the corner that was farthest from him; and they stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:73:He had watched that woman read since a reading room above Ostrand. She read as some people eat, quickly, with her whole attention, and a page was gone before another person had found its first line. She did not read so now; her eyes went along one sign at a time, and her lips were shut. Twice the pencil went to the notebook and wrote a single letter, and went back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:77:She did not take the leaf's word for either. She turned to the last of the tall books, which she had kept by her, and hunted the old clerk's column for a word with the first of the two in its middle; and when she had found one she laid her pencil along both, the brown ink and the scored skin, and nodded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:91:There was one thing more, and she did it with her glove: she laid it over a word near the foot of the skin. On her notebook she wrote out what that word would have to be, sign for sign, if the letters meant at the foot what she had taken them to mean at the head; and then she lifted the glove, and looked from the one to the other, with the pencil gone quite still in her fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:97:"I can read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:99:"Then read it," said the man. His eyes were still shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:105:"Once," she said. "I shall read it once, aloud, as nearly as I can put a dead tongue into ours. I shall not do it twice. It is the first face only; there is more on its back, which is a list, and I have not gone into that." Her voice was low, and quite steady, and it cost her to keep it so. "It has a head, and then it begins."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:107:She set one gloved finger in the air above the first line, an inch off the skin, as a man had taught her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:109:"*In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong*" — her breath had given out upon a comma, and she took another without lifting her finger — "*there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:115:"It is scraped," she said, in her own voice. "A word as wide as four of his letters, or five. I have the first stroke of it, and nothing after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:119:"*— set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it. And this is the reason of the apparatus, and this is all of its reason, and let none who come after forget it, though the un-bound be forgotten: that they should not come again.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:123:She took it away, and did not turn the leaf. She stood with both hands flat on the oak, and looked at the wall of pale rock above the desk, where a pick had left its strokes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:"That is what it says," she said. "I have given it as nearly as I am able, and I shall make a copy, in our tongue, to the letter. Whoever was not here shall have it from the page." She drew a breath. "I ask one thing. I ask that nobody say it back. Not to me; not to each other, in his own words, on a stair. A thing that is said twice begins to be said differently. It is very old, and it is not ours to improve."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:131:The man had not opened his eyes; he stood two paces from his own desk, with his hands hanging, as still as a thing put down. Six strides off, on a box, the ash stick had been lifted a little from the rock, and was held so, and did not come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:137:"I shall say what I take it to be," said Karis. "I shall say it once as well, and plainly, and then I shall stop. It is not the leaf. It is only my account of the leaf, and it is worth less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:147:"That word stands here. It is spelled as he has spelled it, letter for letter, in the old signs. *The Architect.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:159:"The rest is what you heard. It says what the apparatus is for, and it says that there is no other reason. It was set over the land so that a kind of people should not come again." Her voice had dropped. "It calls them the un-bound. I said that word at this table on the first day, as a limit; I had only ever met it as the name of a layer in the ground. I did not know that it had been a people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:163:"That is my whole account. I have read no more than the first face, and I have said no more than is on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:165:She looked down at her right hand, and shut three fingers of it into the palm, one at a time, as a big man had counted findings at a spring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:169:It was three words. Cael counted them, as he counted anything, and found that he could go no further along that road than the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:173:He went over it as he would have gone over a cut on a moor, from the left. There was a desk of black oak, sloped, and a lamp on an iron arm, with the oil a finger down. On the shelf above stood a cup, which he had drunk from, and a jar; and a clean square in the dust. Two lengths of brown tape lay out to either side of a leaf of skin with twenty-six lines on it. There were two chairs, of which one had a cushion that had been carried from the other. A man stood with his eyes shut. A woman stood with a strip of a shirt round her left hand, and the hand was flat on the wood, and it had left off shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:179:He knew what he was doing. He was counting a gallery so that he need not count the one thing in it which the leaf concerned. He let himself. He thought that he would be given the rest of the day to stand in this room; he would not be given it to stand in the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:187:Karis lifted the skin away on its beech, with both hands, the bound and the whole, and set it at the head of the desk's slope. What lay beneath was a dozen leaves or more, of a thick soft paper gone brown at the edges, written in ink in a cramped upright hand. She bent to the first, and her face changed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:189:"I can read this at sight," she said. "It is the old town's hand. It is the hand of the tally-books, or its father's." Her pencil went down the leaf without touching. "It is a chronicle. It has the floods in it, and a murrain among the cattle, and who was reeve. Somebody of this town kept a year-book, and somebody else laid it away with that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:195:She read the line out before she had decided whether to. Cael saw that; her voice ran ahead of her, as it sometimes did when a thing was plain upon the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:There was a hole in the paper. Cael could see it from where he stood: a place the width of a thumb, a little way in from the edge, where the leaf was simply gone. Its rim was brown and soft. A worm had done it, or damp; the line of ink ran up to that rim, and went over it, and did not come out upon the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:205:"No," she said. "That is another day's work, and it is torn. I am not going to read into a hole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:207:She took a slip of card from her notebook, and laid it across the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:219:It came down upon the rock, once, six strides away. The old man had sat through a reading and an account with his hands shut upon it, and his face turned to a bay of bundles; and whatever he had been holding off had got past him. He spoke to the shelves, low, and with care, as he had once begun to draft an order at a spring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:221:"The apparatus," he said. "The measure, the seat, the tier, the mark. He made it and he set it over the land. And the mark that is given at the kindling — "
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:225:She had turned round; it was the first time since the tape was untied that she had looked at anybody, and she looked at him as she had looked across a table at Lowmarch, when a courier's flat lay between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:227:"There is what that leaf says. It says it in its own words, of its own time, and I shall copy it on one page, and nothing else shall go upon that page." She took a clean leaf from her notebook, and laid it on the desk, bare. "And there is this. What you were about to say is not on the skin. It may be so. I do not know, and nor do you. If it is ever written, it will be written here, when somebody can show it; and until then this page has nothing on it, and nobody is to rule a line from the one to the other because a word stands in both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:233:The old mouth had set. Cael watched the right hand leave the stick, and hang, and go back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:237:She had said it at a spring, over six years of an office's sheets, to stop a sentence of her own. She said it now with her bound hand on a bare leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:249:She would not be hurried over it, and nobody tried. She sat down at last, in the second chair, on the cushion that was not hers, with a board across her knees. She wrote the first face of that skin out fair, in our tongue, in her small upright hand, with the leaf before her and her eyes going from one to the other at every word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:251:Cael kept his place at the desk's side, and read it as it came, from the wrong side, with its head away from him, as he had read every paper that had ever concerned him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:259:*and the* — there the box stood, with nothing in it — *set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:261:"I am not going to put a name where a knife has been," she said, without lifting her head. "I am not going to put a title there either. There was a word, and it is gone; and that it is gone is a part of what the leaf says."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:265:It was the first thing that he had said since she began to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:267:"There is. I have drawn it." She turned the board a very little, so that he might see. At the left side of her box, inside the rule, there stood one upright line as tall as a letter. "I do not know what it began."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:269:She finished the face. She laid the pencil down across the board, and flexed her fingers, and looked at what she had done as a mason looks along a course.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:275:It was Brom's tread, and beside it a quicker one. Cael turned his head, and saw the lamp first, low, at Brom's knee, and above it Lira's face with dust on it to the eyebrows; and he saw the face take in that end of the gallery, in the time that it takes to draw a breath, and go still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:279:"Twelve," said Brom. "As they stood. She'd have me move the light for every one." He set the ring down on the table's end, a long way from any paper. His eyes had gone to the desk, and the two beech boards. "It's open, then."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:283:"And read?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:287:Lira had come to his shoulder. She did not look at the skin; she looked at him, as she had looked on a stair by a lamp, going over his face a line at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:307:She looked at him a moment longer; and then, for the first time, at the leaf of horn on its board, from six feet off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:17:"I can count as far as three," said Karis. "I have the figures for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:19:He made a sound in his chest that was nearly a laugh; it was the first that Cael had heard out of him, and it seemed to surprise him as much as anybody. He wrote the card for the morning with his nose an inch from the pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:21:The clerk's bell went, small and flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:23:They roped the boxes. Brom took his on the right shoulder, as he had for four days, and Lira got her arms through her beckets, the right and then the left. Cael felt the third box settle between his shoulder-blades, with a purse in it under five thumbs. The old man hooked the tarred case with the head of his stick, and brought it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:25:At the iron the man in the dusty coat drew the bar and stood back. His red eyes were on their boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:"Second bell," he said, as he said every evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:39:Cael looked up, at the roof of the last building, where a parapet ran along above the slot in the wall. There was nothing on it. There had been nothing on it for two days, morning and evening, and he had learned to read that as a small grey man doing his work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:41:The gate at the cut's foot stood open, as it always stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:43:They went into it in the order that the stair had taught them. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, with the stick going up a tread ahead of him and the bound leg swung after; and Cael came two steps below, where a man would fall. Lira followed, with her arms through her beckets. Karis was behind her, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; and Brom came last, and his box all but brushed both walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:57:It did not swing to, as a gate does in a wind. It was put to, hard, and a bar went down across it; and the sound came up the slot like a blow on a cask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:63:They stood with their backs to it. They wore long coats of no colour, and hoods, and each had a cloth tied across the lower half of his face, so that there was nothing under the hood at all. Each had a stave. They were not porters' poles. They were as long as a man and as thick as a wrist, and shod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:67:Cael's breath had stopped. He had ten things in him, and every one had come awake at that bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:71:It was the smallest sound in the slot: a ferrule grating round on stone, a step and a half above his head. He was looking downhill, as they all were. He did not at once understand why the old man was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:One knelt on the highest landing, with a thing laid along his forearm that Cael knew before he could see it plainly. It was short, and it had a bow across its end. A second stood over him with a stave. A third was already coming down the flight below them, in the middle of the steps, as a man walks who knows that the ground is his. And at the very top, where the slot opened beside the parapet, the fourth stood by himself. He had one hand on a bar of iron that was let into the wall there for people coming up under loads. In the other he held a lantern, low, against his coat, with its shutter closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:87:Cael saw the bow come up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:91:It struck the edge of the ninth step, once, with the whole of a tall man's weight upon it. The sound was not the sound of ash on stone. He had heard it on a moor. A line went out from the ferrule to either wall, along the lip of the tread, as a crack runs across ice. Then the air stood up from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:95:It roared. He could not have said how a thing roars that makes no noise. He stood two steps beneath it, with an Arbiter in his breast that had been dark since the day it woke, and it came over him all the same, as the heat comes out of a forge when the door is thrown back. It was going out of the man steadily, as it had gone out of him on a road, with nothing kept in hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:115:He had turned at the bar, with the rest, and he had not turned back. Cael did not see the beginning of it. He had it afterward from three people, and from the stair. He saw the end, over his shoulder, between one breath and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:117:Brom let his box go. He did not set it down; he had no arm that would have set it. He dropped his right shoulder, and the thing slid off the webbing and fell across the fourth step with its long side to the gate, and lay there. It shut one half of the slot. He stood in the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:121:"That's far enough," he said to the two with staves, exactly as he would have said it to a carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:129:He was off it, and had it before it could fall back. He could not raise his arms. He could bend, and he bent, and got both hands under a slab of the dark stone as long as his own forearm and a hand thick; and he took it up as far as his thighs, on his legs and his back, as he had taken a dray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:137:Brom had not straightened. Something had gone in his left shoulder as the stone left him, deeper than what the dray had done, and he was bent over it with his teeth showing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:143:She had been on the second flight, two steps under Cael's heels, with a box on her back and a small pale woman between her and the fight. She had got out of her beckets. Cael never learned how; the box was found afterward standing square on the seventh step, as if she had set it there for a seat. She went down past Karis, who had pressed herself to the wall, and took the two strides that the landing gave her; and she did not go on down the flight. She stepped off its head into the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:145:It was not far, as she reckoned falls: the height of a table, and the length of a man outward. She came down over Brom's box, with her knees drawn up and the long knife in her right hand, and her left arm was folded against her ribs as tight as a wing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:151:A stave cannot be swung with a girl upon it. It came down between them, slowly, like a gate-bar, and its shod end struck the tread and stayed. The man had a hand free. He let go with it, and reached for her hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:163:Karis had flattened herself to let the girl go by. She had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them up and down that stair for seven days, and she had not a hand to spare for the wall. Her boot was on the sixth step. It was the wet one, worn into a dish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:165:It went from under her. She sat down hard on the stair, and the flat did not leave her arm. It was her left hand that went out to save the rest of her, and found the lip of the step above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:171:"The flat is dry," she said, in a voice that was not quite steady. "I should like that known."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:185:It was the hardest thing that he had done on that stair. Everybody that he had was behind him: a girl going through the air, and a big man with no arms, and a small one with a courier's flat. He heard the stone go down, and the gate take a man's back. He heard a knife go into wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:189:Wind was first, as it had always been, the dry hiss in his feet. *Away?* it said; and there was nowhere on that stair that was away. Under it the millstone went round, and told him the weight of a slab that he could not see, and which way it was falling. Ember ticked in his right hand, and showed him a cord, ten yards off, drawn back along a stock; a single point would have gone through that cord as through a thread. Pressure had drawn itself as tight as a held breath. A slot between two walls was the very thing that it was made for; it would have gone up that stair like a cork up the neck of a bottle. *There?* asked the thin held note, of the place by the parapet where the slot began; and a point set there would have shut the head of the cut like a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:193:He told each of them no. He did it one at a time, as a man goes along a row of dogs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:199:Nobody brings a light to a beating and keeps it dark. That man had been put there to show it when a certain thing happened. It had not happened yet. He was waiting for it, and so were the other three; the one with the bow had not laid a second bolt on his string.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:203:They had been told. Somebody had stood these six in a place with no way out of it, and had given them a bow to frighten with and staves to hurt with; and all that they wanted in return was to see what a boy with no tier did when he was hurt enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:205:Then they should see him do nothing, from as near as he could bring it. A wall wanted a reason behind it; he would be the reason, and stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:An old man had said a thing at a board, on a moor, with his bowl set down upon a sill, about a wall and what it wants behind it. Cael had been that once, by going ten strides off and landing like a post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:211:It put him on the eighth tread, at a brown coat's left hip, in the open half of the slot, where there was nothing between the bow and himself but a place that the eye slid off. He stood square. He lifted his face, so that the last of the light was on it, and looked up the stair at four hoods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:213:The bow came round to him at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:221:"You need not have come up," said Vastin, without turning his head. His voice was thin, and quite steady. "It will hold as well for one as for two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:229:He came across it without hurrying, and stopped where the air had bloomed. He put up a gloved hand. He laid the palm of it flat on nothing, a yard from Cael's face, as a man lays a hand on a window to learn whether there is glass in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:231:He pushed. He set his shoulder to it, once, with his weight behind, and Cael heard the knee take that as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:233:Then he stood back by half a pace, with his palm still upon the plane, and looked through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:239:It was the quietest minute that he had ever stood through. There was noise below him, of stone and timber and people's breath, and he could do nothing about any of it. In front of him four people had gone as still as he had. They were waiting for a boy to be whatever they had been told that he was; and the boy stood on a step, with a box on his back, and gave them a porter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:243:He felt what it was, from two feet beneath it, with nothing to do but feel. Every one of his ten went somewhere. The hiss ran, and the millstone turned, and the point ticked, and the bellows drew; even the thing that read went along with whatever it read. This did not go. It had been told to stay, and it stayed, with an old man's whole strength poured down into it like water into sand. It was a heel set upon a line, and meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:247:Something in him set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:249:It was not as the others had come. Tide had filled him like a cup, under a healer's hand. This did not fill. It was as a post is stood in its hole: not driven, but put there, and the earth trodden down about it a heel at a time, until a person could have hung a gate on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:255:It asked of the step in front of his boots, in a voice like a bar going down into two sockets. He knew what it was before he knew that it was new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:263:The hooded man had taken it off as a person takes his hand from a door that he has decided not to open. He turned his head, up the stair, and lifted two fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:271:Cael saw it only because he was looking at the lantern. It was no thicker than a bootlace. It came down from the roof's edge, above the slot's mouth, with something small and heavy at its end that swung once, round an arm and a bar of iron together, three times, quicker than he could follow, and bit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:273:The lantern jerked sideways. It struck the rail, and fell on the top step with its shutter still closed, and rolled, and went out inside itself with a small flat cough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:281:Then he whistled. It was two low notes, such as a carter gives a horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:283:It ended as quietly as it had begun. The man with the bow rose from his knee, and unstrung the thing, and put it under his coat. The one beside him went up to the rail, and drew a knife, and cut a thin cord in two places; and the fourth took up his dead lantern. None of them ran. They went into the lane, one after another, like people who have come to the wrong door, and the hood on the landing went last. He walked up the two flights backward, with his face to the plane, and at the top he stood for a moment and looked down it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:287:Below, a bar was lifted. Cael heard the gate swing, and two pairs of boots on the paving of a yard, going away at a walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:289:He counted. He had been counting since the stick came down, as he counted anything, and he was at eighty-one. It had been a minute and some.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:299:There was no frost to fall. The place in front of him that the eye had slid off was only a stair again, with two bolts lying on it; and the roar went out of the slot as a fire goes out when the door is shut on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:303:It did not bend. It let go, sideways, all at once, as a prop is knocked from under a beam. The whole long weight of him came over backward, with the case and the stick, onto the step where Cael stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:He told it no. He took him as a porter would have. He got both arms round a brown coat, under the shoulders, and the box on his own back dragged him the way that they were both going. His left heel lost the tread, and found the next. They went down two steps so, the pair of them, and a tarred case came after and struck him on the ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:309:Something stopped them. It was as hard as a gatepost, and it had four yards of webbing round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:311:"I've got you," said Brom, from below, between his teeth. He had his right arm across Cael's back. It was the one that a healer had said was to carry nothing. "Stand still, the pair of you. I've only the one, and it's the wrong one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:7:The woman of the half-door came out to look at it with a candle. She looked at the wheels as well, and at the floor of the end stall, where an old man had folded his blanket in four and laid it on the manger. She said that she had kept lodgers over those horses for eleven years, and had never before been handed back a stall tidier than she had let it. Then she counted them out under the arch by their feet, as she had counted them in, and went back to her bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:11:The rope-walk was awake before the street was. A lantern burned at the head of the long shed, where the wheel stood, and another far down it, as small as a star; and between the two a line of people walked backward in the half dark, with the yarn running out between their fingers. Brom went along the pegs as he had gone along them thirteen days before. He did not need to hunt. He stopped at the coil that he had stopped at then, and took a fathom off it, and bent the end back, and looked in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:19:"You hung my fathom back as you found it. I've sold rope in this shed to men who'd not do as much for their mothers." She had the coil down already, across her two arms. "Sixty yards. It's the price that I named."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:27:"I can't," he said. "I've been turning it over since the fire. It's the weight of half a man, and it'll not go on a back that's got a box on it already." He said it without shame, as he would have said that a plank was short. "I asked you last night who hauls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:31:There was a dray in the lane. It was a long low thing on four wheels, with two brown cobs in its shafts, and it was loaded to the rails with coils, tarred and white, under a sheet that had not yet been lashed down. A lean man with a straw in his mouth was going round it, trying each knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:33:"That's for the western landing," said the woman. "Three days down the moor road. There's a broker there who's sold three-strand for as long as I've laid it, and a fortnight since he sent up for four. Forty coils. He wrote that a big man from over the water had stood in front of his stack with a full purse, and called his rope the wrong kind, and gone away with the purse; and that he'd not have it said to him twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:39:"He's taken it hard, whatever you said. That's forty coils that I'd not have laid." She jerked her chin at the lean man. "It's his road, and you'll ask him. But I'd think that a coil could ride with its own kind, to the place where it's wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:41:The carter had heard. He came round the tail of the dray and looked Brom over slowly, from the boots upward, as a man reads a waybill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:53:"That walks as far as he's able," said Cael. "After that he doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:65:The gate was opened at first light, as two painted boards had promised, and they were in the arch with the first of the carts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The keeper in undyed grey sat on his high stool with the frame on his knee. He looked at feet, and wheels, and hooves, as he had looked at them thirteen days before, and his thumb moved the beads: eight hooves, and four wheels. He came to the boots, and Cael counted five pairs with him. The thumb went on to the tail-board, where a pair of boots hung that did not touch the ground, and it stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:73:The keeper looked at her, for the first time. It was plain that nobody argued with that frame from one cold term to the next. Then he moved two beads along the upper wire, slowly, as a man pays a debt that he had hoped was forgotten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:83:He counted the piers. He had counted them coming in, twice, since Karis would ask; he counted them now for a reason of his own. There were five. They stood in the water like the knuckles of a hand laid flat, each with its bay above it where a person on foot might stand aside. The thing behind his eyes had come round to the first of them before his boot was on the roadway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:89:It was his own count, and it was made with his own eyes, which could not do two things at once. The lean came up short against it, as a dog comes up against a door that has been shut without unkindness. At the third pier the grit had not come. At the fifth he knew that he had found a thing which he could use. He had spent his life counting what was in front of him so that he need not feel it, and for once the habit was worth something to somebody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:93:She had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; and her bound hand lay open on the parapet's dark cap, palm down, in its linen. She was not measuring anything. She only stood, with her head a little on one side, and looked along the roadway at two grooves that were deeper than a wheel's rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:99:"I have not got a week, and I have not got a sheet for it." She took the hand away. "I know more about what stood here before the walls than any person living. I have it from a skin, and a board, and three floors that are a week east of this. I have not so much as drawn the thing that I walked over to reach them. I should like that entered against me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:111:He could stand in this bay, as any carter might, and look back up the whole of that hill to the top of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:119:"Five," he said aloud, to Lira. "It's still five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:125:The road ran on its causeway for three miles, with a ditch on either hand, and a white mist stood in the river meadows as high as the cobs' bellies.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:127:It was a town's ground. A person might speak on it, as far as the first hedge, and for a long while nobody did. The dray set the pace, which was a stick's pace or a little better. The lean man walked at his near cob's head, and the six came after his wheels in the order that they had learned, with Seln gone on ahead into the white. Cael walked last, behind the tail-board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:135:It stood at the western end of a carters' pull-in, where a hedge stopped and a bank ran on beside the ditch. Cael knew that bank. A man had let himself down upon it, in the dusk, where he could not rise again without a boy's arm, and had said why he once declined to read that boy a second time. They had called it ground afterward. It had not been friendship, and it was not now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:137:*No*, he told the thing. It had asked at a milestone, as it asked at every line that anybody had ever drawn across a road. The man whom it had come out of looked over his head at a town, and did not know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:143:"It looks like a hill with three walls on it. I had supposed that it would look like more." He considered it a while longer. "I was eleven days in the bottom of it, as a hand, on a card that I could not have signed. I find that I knew it better from a stall than I ever did from the registrar's table. I do not say so to be thought humble. It is a finding, and it does not flatter the office."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:145:Cael said nothing to that. He had the fair copy of a leaf inside his coat, against a pair of brass legs; and he had two things under his breastbone that the man in front of him had never been told of, of which one was the man's own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:169:"It's the same face." She had slipped her left arm out of its becket while she spoke. The box hung from the right shoulder alone, and she let the left hand take the other becket low, at her hip, and carry there. "Thirty. That's her month. I've been slow for the whole of it, as I was told to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:179:It was Seln who put it on. He had been thirty yards ahead of the cobs all the morning. Where the last laid hedge gave out, and the ruts ran up onto open ground, he stood in the road with his right hand flat at his side, palm down, until the whole file had seen it. Nobody needed the words. The lean man spat out his straw. The girl who had talked for three miles about a broker's face shut her mouth on the rest, and every sigil on that road went into a pocket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:181:Cael felt the twelve of them settle, as hounds settle at a threshold that they know. Wind had his feet already. The read asked, civilly, what the ruts weighed. He went round the house and shut the doors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:187:He did it as he had learned to do everything in the last fortnight, by his hands, a little at a time, with the sound foot finding the road first and the stick after it. The case he left on the coils. Nobody offered him an arm, since he would have had to refuse it aloud. But Brom, with a box on his shoulder, had fallen back to walk on his right, and Cael was on his left, as he had been once before on this very road, going the other way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:189:It was a mile and more to the top, aslant, with the wind coming over it into their faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:197:The dray had stopped there, to let the cobs blow, and the lean man had a hand on a wet neck. Behind them, a long way below, lay a green plain with hedges on it, and a river in loops; and in the nearest loop a bluff stood up with three walls about it in steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:203:He did it on the stick, the whole of him, since the knee would not let him do it by halves. He stood on the crown of the road with the east wind in the skirts of the brown coat and looked back, for the space of three breaths, at the last town on the Line. He had certified it four times under his own seal; and he had left that seal in a tray, a long way south of here, with nothing written to go beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:209:The fork-stone stood at the rise's eastern foot, as tall as Brom, with a worn hand cut into each of its faces. One hand pointed along the ruts by which they had come. The other pointed north, where a second pair went away under the moor's edge toward a line of blue hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:Brom did not stop. But as he went by the stone he put his left hand up, as far as it would go, which was to his own chest; and he laid two fingers on a thing that rode under the lashing of his box, which was a wooden bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:217:After the fork the moor took them, and there was nothing to do for six miles but walk into the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:219:It was a country for people who had made their peace with one colour. The swells went away eastward, brown beyond brown, with the ruts drawn over them like a pair of pencil lines; there was no tree, and no wall, and the only thing that stood higher than a sheep was the dray. The wind came out of the east steadily, with nothing in it but cold. It found the gap between a collar and a neck. It leaned on a box as a hand leans, and by the second mile Cael had the ache of it across both shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:223:Nobody had spoken since the hedge. He had forgotten in thirteen days how loud that silence was. A town's noise is a blanket; a road's quiet is a room with every door open, and a person who carries twelve things that would each like to answer has the minding of all the doors. The quietest of them would lift at dusk. He could feel where it lay, as a person feels where the cold place is in a bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:227:He went over that as he walked, since the road asked for nothing but his feet. There had been a boy on this moor fourteen days ago with a date in him that he had not written down, and a page that he had not touched, and a promise in a book. That boy had been afraid of a gate. He had thought that the worst thing a town could do was to write his name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:229:It had not written it. It had done a good deal more than that. It had let him hold a lamp for ten days over the oldest paper in the country; and at the end of the ten, a small pale woman had read out, once, what the whole of it had been built against. He had two things more in him than he had carried up this road, and a paper in his coat, and a sentence that he had said aloud by a fire. He was the same weight on the scale. He did not think that he was the same load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:235:The outcrop came up out of the east a little after the middle of the afternoon: a block of the dark plated stone as big as a barn, a furlong south of the road, with a thread of green going away from its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:237:The carter knew it. He turned his cobs off the ruts without a sign from anybody, as a man turns in at his own gate; and the dray went rocking over the bents toward a basin that somebody had built round with stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:243:The wind was in the east today.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:245:It came straight along the outcrop's face, with nothing between. The slab lay as it had lain, grey and level and bare; and a dead stem of bracken went over it while they watched, end over end, and off the far side. There was a row of pebbles yet at one edge, set out by size. Two of the smallest had been blown down into the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:247:Karis looked at it for some time, with the flat under her arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:She sat down on one of the plates, with her back to the stone, and laid the flat across her knees, and did not undo its straps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:257:He had turned his cobs out on the green by the overflow. He took the seventh bowl from Brom as a thing that the road owed him, and he had it empty before the old man had begun; and then he lay down under his own tail-board with his cap over his eyes. Nobody lit anything. It was a furlong off the road, and a person might have spoken there. Nobody did that either, beyond what the bowls wanted. They had six miles of wind in their ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:263:He stood at the water's edge with his hands in his sleeves and his back to the slab, looking down into the basin; and it was so exactly where he had stood on the two hundred and eightieth day that Cael's foot stopped of itself. He had been at that slab then, with a row of paper under his hands that counted a sheet short. He had looked up, and found a small grey back turned to him at the water. And when he looked down again there had been a sheet lying before him on the rock, which had not been there, with a registry year at its head and a day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:267:He had written none of it. He had said none of it, to a healer or a girl or a man with a lamp. In the fifteen days since, a leaf had been read aloud in a gallery which made a good many things look different, and he had not once let himself carry that sheet across to stand beside it. That was for the clean page, if it was for any; and a woman had laid that page down bare, with a bound hand, and had said that nobody was to rule a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:271:Cael knelt at the basin's lip, upwind, and dipped the pot. The water was as cold as a knife. Neither of them said one word. He did not know what that man had made of a date, and he had never asked. It came to him, on his knees in the bents, that he was not the only person at this spring who kept a thing out of his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:275:Karis had not opened the flat. But she had the reader's roll of card out of her coat, with its turn of brown tape; and beside it, on her knee, her own notebook at the fourth line. She sat bent between the two, in what was left of the light, with her bound hand holding the one flat and the pencil going in the other; and her lips were moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:277:"Six," she said, under her breath, as he went by. It was not said to him. "The first. The fourth. And the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:He went, and sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book. The brass legs slid from it into his lap, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for three days and had not once opened them upon a distance. A ruling of his own stood in the way, and it was a hundred and twenty-seven days old. Whatever measured had read one way in four hands and another in his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:289:*Known: a carter at a rope-walk knew Brom by a thing that a broker said three days' road from there. I have been written down since I was fourteen. It is not the same as being known, and I do not yet know what it weighs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:293:The light was gone before he had ruled the line. Shadow lifted in him, as it lifted at every dusk, and the other eleven dropped away under it like a yard going quiet under snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:5:She had not struck a point for it; Brom had lit the thing at the kitchen fire, with a spill, as he did every evening now, and it gave a brown uneven light that she would once have been ashamed to work by. She had got used to it in six nights, and it appeared that a person could get used to a great deal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He had said another thing, three days before, from his chair, with his eyes shut, and had said it of himself. She had not answered him. She had stood at the Hall's table and known it for her own as well. She had walked into a cook-house with fifty-two sheets and a lifetime of being smiled at by better-placed people, and a stranger had cleared three tables and given her his cushion and told her that he knew the hand; and she had not understood until then how light a pencil is in the fingers of a person whom nobody attends to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:15:So she would say nothing next. She would read a number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:17:She turned back a leaf, to where she had copied the plank's four rows that forenoon, squared to a hand's breadth, with the lamp held low upon the left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:23:There were six signs after the first twelve, and four after the nine. After the second twelve there were six again; and they were the same six, sign for sign, in the same order, as had followed the first; and after the seven there were five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:A careful man writes a sum twice, once in figures and once in words, so that the one may be checked against the other, and a clerk at any counter did it still. If this cutter had done it, then those little groups were the names of his numbers; and if they were, each sign in them was not a thing that bore or hung. It was a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:It was a handsome idea, and it explained why two twelves were followed by the same six signs. It did not explain it any better than three others that she could think of. The six might be a maker's mark, set on every full row. They might say *checked*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:33:She took a clean leaf, and headed it in her small upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:37:If the groups were number-words, they were words in some tongue, and she did not know the tongue; but there were not many that it could be. The stone of that archive was older than the town's first wall, and the oldest paper in it would be the nearest in age to whoever had cut the board. She had been told that the old town's first tally-books stood in the eastern gallery, on shelves that she had not yet seen. A tally-book wrote its numbers out in full, as every clerk's did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:That was the whole of the method, and she set it out in three lines. From the old hand's own spelling of *twelve*, and of *nine*, and of *seven*, she would write which letters in each were alike, and which letter of one word stood also in another; a pattern of same and different, and nothing more. She would fold that leaf; then she would carry it to the plank, and lay it beside the cutting, and see whether the signs repeated where the letters did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:53:Karis had asked for it at the desk, before the man had turned his first page. He had told her the bay without looking round: the third on the left, the tall books, as high as the roof. He had said that the lamp might go there and come back, and that it was not to be set down on any shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:63:It was as broad as a paving stone. It came down the ladder against a thin chest, a rung at a time, and it was put into Karis's arms with a cloud of that pale dust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:67:It was a tally of loads, as she had known that it would be. A cramped brown hand had written, line under line, what had come in at some gate and what had been paid on it; and the hand wrote every figure out, in full, in the old spelling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:69:She found *twelve* within the first leaf, and *nine*, twice, spelled alike both times. *Seven* took her longer; this town had brought in a great many things by the dozen and the score, and few by sevens.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:73:On her folded leaf she wrote the three words as that clerk had written them, one under another. Then she wrote the pattern, as she had sworn to, and nothing else. In the first word, six letters; the third and the sixth alike. In the second, four; the first and the third alike, and its last the same as that pair in the word above. In the third, five; its second and fourth alike, and the same again as that letter; its third the same as the fifth of the first word; its last the same as the first of the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:75:She read it over. It was a dull little table of sameness, such as a child makes of a counting-rhyme, and it had no sign in it, and no wish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:81:She laid the leaf on the rock at the board's foot. She put the point of her pencil in the air above the first group, the six signs that followed two rings and two pecks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:83:The third sign was a small upright with a hook. She went along. The sixth was an upright with a hook. She set the pencil's point under the one and the tip of her little finger under the other, and had the pair of them between the two ends of her own hand: third and last, as the old clerk's *twelve* had them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:85:Her hand had begun to shake, and she stopped, and held it with the other until it was still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:89:She did not let herself breathe out. The seven. Five signs. The second was the hooked upright, and so was the fourth. The third was a sign like a fork. She kept her pencil on it, and said *up*; and the boy carried the light up the plank to the first row, where the fifth of the six stood out of the dark, and it was a fork. He brought the flame down again without being told. The last was a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:93:She sat back on her heels, and the rock was very cold through her skirt, and the lamp stood up beside her like a painted one, and she could hear the man at the desk turn a leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:95:It was a tongue. It was the old town's, or that tongue's grandmother; and a man who had cut orders into the floors of three buried places had counted his rows in it, as a carter counts sacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:107:She wrote them in a column on a fresh leaf, each sign and the sound that it stood for. The hooked upright. The gate. The fork. There were eight when she had done, from three small words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:115:She was on the floor against the table's leg, with her bread, and she said it as she would have told somebody with a key to open a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:161:She saw them now on every sheet, and had no means of not seeing them. One stood four times in the Stair's twenty-nine: six signs, always the same six, always after a white. She had four of its letters. She had turned the old tongue over in her head, before she could stop herself, and found a word that would lie in those four as a hand lies in a glove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:165:She did not write it, and she did not say it, even in the place behind her eyes where she said things first; she turned the notebook's leaf over upon it and went back to the tally-book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:169:One lay on a shelf above the desk, beside a jar, tied across both ways in brown tape. He had put two fingers on it and told her what it was made of; she had wanted it from that moment as she had once wanted a page with four signs upon it, and she had not asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:Both could wait until she could read; she had waited a good deal longer for less.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:193:He did not wait for the pot; he stood in the round of the lantern, with his hands in his sleeves, and he gave it as he would have given a count of days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:197:Brom set his box down very slowly, with his knees.

