+# Book 8 listening proof — part 3 reading notes
+
+Scope: `manuscript/chapter-31.md` through `chapter-45.md`. Each chapter was printed whole with line numbers and read in full, in order. The boundary snippets below are the first and last six whitespace-delimited words of every scene, copied from the manuscript; markdown marks remain where they fall in those words. No manuscript file was modified, and no git command was run.
+
+## Chapter 31 — One at a Time — 5 scenes
+
+1. `Karis sat up that night against` … `since a store behind a chimney.` — Karis's private method and the figure/word reasoning remain clear aloud; no unattributed speech.
+2. `The book came down from the` … `and went back to his page.` — Lane join at line 71 is ruled sound in the dedicated join section below. Cleon's rough-voice questions, Karis's answers and the unnamed lamp-boy remain distinct.
+3. `"Read the rest, then," said Lira,` … `and he bent to his page.` — The ten-letter work and Cleon's closing `Good` are attributable; the number sequence is coherent.
+4. `Seln was at the ladder's foot` … `"No," said Lira. "Nor did you."` — Six-person loft council is audible: Seln gives the surveillance finding, Brom supplies the card inference, Vastin answers his earlier failure, Cael rules, and Lira closes privately.
+5. `Brom paid at the half-door in` … `will be wanted in the morning.` — Purse moves to a hundred and thirty-four; Lira's ladder practice and Cael's anticipatory lamp movement are clear. Clean.
+
+## Chapter 32 — No Round — 5 scenes
+
+1. `The fourteenth letter came out of` … `at the foot, the gate shut.` — The party's stair order and the gate closure are spatially clear; the old man is Vastin throughout.
+2. `It did not swing to, as` … `that place there is no round.` — Fight setup is clear by position: two below, four above, Vastin facing uphill, one crossbow, one dark lantern.
+3. `It was Brom who met the` … `on the other end of it.` — Brom, Lira and Karis each retain a separate action line; `the flat` is the courier's flat, not flat ground.
+4. `Cael had turned back uphill before` … `the eleventh time, in that stair.` — Every refusal is explicit. The eleventh's first voice, *This far?*, follows the still wall rather than a deployment and cannot be mistaken for spoken dialogue.
+5. `The hand came away from the` … `one, and it's the wrong one."` — Seln's cord intervention, the six attackers' withdrawal and Cael's count of eighty-one are clear. Long paragraph at line 189 is listed in the proof.
+
+## Chapter 33 — Entered — 5 scenes
+
+1. `They let the old man down` … `to explain that card to anybody."` — Injury roll is clear: Lira right shoulder, Brom both shoulders, Karis left palm, Vastin knee. Seln's mild voice enters from above without ambiguity.
+2. `There was a house inside the` … `glad that it was the left."` — Treatment scene is audible: the registered healer is rejected because the name would be entered; Brom sews seven stitches; Lira holds the light.
+3. `Vastin could not hold a bowl.` … `close its hand upon a bowl.` — The eleventh acquisition unit is intact and wholly italic. `Eleven.` gives the narrator a clean landing after the notice.
+4. `"I should be obliged for a` … `can't hold a cup," said Lira.` — Vastin's three-headed account stays one formal voice. The protected `I put the wall…` / `It's entered` exchange is attributable, and the debt discussion distinguishes permission to tell from permission to take.
+5. `Seln came down off the ladder's` … `which way it would be wanted.` — Seln's finding, the log, initials `V.` and `S.`, deployment thirty-two and the protected eleventh entry all flatten intelligibly in document voice.
+
+## Chapter 34 — The Carts' Street — 5 scenes
+
+1. `They let the old man down` … `what it costs when I do."` — Yard council and card arithmetic are clear. Seln's repeated rules are recognizably quoted back, not a new speaker.
+2. `A handcart went up a hill` … `rattled out onto the carts' street.` — Gate counts `one, and four` remain coherent; Vastin's role as matter on Karis's card is audible.
+3. `It was a road that a` … `be spared the trouble of deciding."` — Beam-man is the only unnamed speaker. `Whose?` is answered by Karis; Vastin's restrained sentence does not blur into hers.
+4. `The clerk had the green book` … `without a word, for the ash.` — Clerk, dusty-coated keeper and Vastin are separated by location and tags; the twenty-seven-step descent is clear.
+5. `The eleventh had nothing to do` … `two things laid foot to foot.` — Cleon's quoted `What's that? / Which lays? / Hm` is framed as recalled speech. No broken join after the lane's restored `old man` wording at line 177.
+
+## Chapter 35 — Lean — 6 scenes
+
+1. `The tall book lay open on` … `they cost. It was the twelfth.` — Cleon's clipped work speech is clear even with his eyes shut; `An instruction leans`, `This is a stair` and Karis's private `The strata lean too` have distinct owners.
+2. `"Red," said the rough voice. "At` … `like a spoon on a cup.` — Cleon and Karis alternate cleanly. The protected Oryn quotation *Write it down and stop touching it* is memory, not a new room voice.
+3. `Vastin had gone to the stair` … `gallery, with no lamp at all.` — Stair council remains attributable among Cael, Lira, Brom and Karis; the statement `It's six people's` is clearly Lira's.
+4. `It was a hundred and ten` … `plank, as he said every evening.` — Cael's disclosure, Cleon's self-correction and protected warning are all clear. Restored protected join is ruled below.
+5. `Brom was still on the box,` … `have known one face from another.` — Short Brom/Cael exchange is clear; Vastin's voice then enters above on the stair.
+6. `It began on the last flight.` … `did not tell anyone. He remembered.` — Twelfth acquisition is intact and wholly italic; the two short italic addenda remain separate. Eye-cost onset and Vastin's step-by-step guidance are clear.
+
+## Chapter 36 — First Bell — 6 scenes
+
+1. `His eyes came back at the` … `you carry that likes the dark."` — Logs are coherent; initials `S.` and `V.` are letter names. Council of six is attributable, with Seln's long formal sentences distinct from Cael and Lira.
+2. `They went out by the door` … `he had begun to count them.` — Seln's following lesson is clear. `Wind` is WIND, and Shadow is silent fragment pressure rather than an external voice.
+3. `The lane went down toward the` … `hundred and ten the boards spoke.` — The restored Wind refusal at line 191 is sound and prevents the burst count from sounding like an action taken; full context below.
+4. `The boots came down the last` … `in, and the small door shut.` — The hooded watcher and Seln remain distinct; no unclear handoff in the quarrying/ferry inference.
+5. `"I'm going up," said Cael. "To` … `brazier, and went down the street.` — Keeper at the blue gate speaks only once; the iron groove makes the eleventh's question audible as inward fragment speech.
+6. `Seln had not stirred from the` … `and slept like a thing dropped.` — Seln's conclusion and Cael's reply are clear. `291st` is a visible italic log mark, not narration; read “two hundred and ninety-first.”
+
+## Chapter 37 — How Far — 7 scenes
+
+1. `The bridge was a made thing` … `if you know how that's done."` — Karis's letter count rises to twenty-one; Cleon's rough voice and Brom's answer are clear. `set` is the decoded old-language word, not a stage direction.
+2. `There was a mule in the` … `"Then keep me what will keep."` — Carrier, Brom, Lira, Cael and Karis remain distinct; Hesk's italic note is introduced before it is read.
+3. `It was Brom's ruling, and he` … `he could reach, and filled it.` — Dinner count, purse reduction to a hundred and thirty, Seln's burned slip and Vastin/Cael ledger exchange are clear. Restored protected exchange ruled below.
+4. `Karis had her twenty-first twice when` … `woman's notebook to remind him why.` — Cael's inventory is intact; the fragments, sources, deployments and anomaly flatten as a single document register.
+5. `The line moved while the brass` … `done it. "It's good for something."` — The claim/evidence/ruling log and the protected promise are intact. The unstable light is one count only and is not promoted to agency.
+6. `On the evening of that same` … `been willing to try it without.` — Oryn's conversation with the old keeper-woman is unambiguous; `May I?` is not present because no treatment occurs.
+7. `The floor was of beaten clay,` … `went to see to the mule.` — Kindling observation is clear and season-blind. Oryn's future consent plan is explicit.
+
+## Chapter 38 — Read Once — 5 scenes
+
+1. `The tall books went back first.` … `"I've looked," he said. "It's yours."` — Four room occupants are fixed before the leaf opens. Cleon's read is distinct from Karis's coming translation.
+2. `She did not sit. She stood` … `it is not ours to improve."` — Karis reads the leaf once. Italics inside quotation marks flatten cleanly; her roman explanation at the scraped gap clearly interrupts the quoted record and then returns to it.
+3. `For a while nobody did anything.` … `toward the bundle. "Under the board."` — Lane join at line 135 is sound; `And Karis` resolves the next speaker after red and grey eyes. The Architect title and the three-word finding are clear.
+4. `It was paper, under the second` … `hear the end of it myself."` — Chronicle line and physical hole remain unglossed. `Marked` is Karis first, Cleon second. Vastin's apparatus inference is stopped by Karis's `Two pages` before any line is ruled between them.
+5. `She made the copy before she` … `"I'll hold you to the page."` — The italic copy with a roman gap flattens in correct order. Lira's questions belong to her; Cael's permission limit is explicit.
+
+## Chapter 39 — Aimed — 5 scenes
+
+1. `Vastin had been given three words` … `look at without wishing to revise.` — Vastin's cutaway is one voice; *imperfect*, *designed* and *aimed* are conceptual beats. The coordinator's applied fix `He would do it again, knowing;` joins soundly to the rest of the sentence.
+2. `He asked for the stair a` … `it up for the last three.` — Vastin's formal voice with self-correction remains clear; Cael answers only the safety question.
+3. `"They lie tonight," the man had` … `lantern was waiting in the vault.` — Cleon's quoted direction is framed before the stair sequence; Lira's `Is that it?` exchange is clear.
+4. `Seln was at the bar. He` … `made his stroke without another word.` — Seln and Cael's compressed paraphrase of the leaf obeys the no-repeat rule and is attributable.
+5. `The stable kept an iron basket` … `and put it into her hand.` — Yard-fire staging fixes every seat and speaker before chapter 40 begins; Cleon's arrival through the yard cannot be confused with the hooded watcher.
+
+## Chapter 40 — The Yard Fire — 5 scenes
+
+1. `Lira read it with her chin` … `answer. "Give it here," said Brom.` — Lira owns both remarks around the retained silence join; Brom's handoff is explicit. The protected existence line remains exact.
+2. `Brom read it once, with the` … `had been asking for an hour.` — Brom, Karis and Seln each take a clearly framed turn. Document custody moves Lira → Brom → Seln → Cael without confusion.
+3. `"Is it true?" said Lira. She` … `otherwise, and it was not there.` — Lira questions Cleon; Cleon answers; Vastin then speaks from the mounting block and Lira answers him. Protected fire exchange is audible.
+4. `That left one. Cael had known` … `most useful thing that you carry."` — Cael's pole-two statement, Lira's tagless `Yeah` and `Now what`, and Cael's plan remain unmistakable by the setup and the explicit attribution after `Yeah`.
+5. `The man in the dusty coat` … `was no line on the boards.` — Cleon's `Tomorrow` direction is clear; logs flatten cleanly; no broken closing join.
+
+## Chapter 41 — The List of Parts — 5 scenes
+
