editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:17:They put Vastin on the mounting block, with the leg out along it. He looked at the ladder for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:53:"I am aware of what I am on that card," said Vastin. "I do not propose to be the reason that it is read twice." He looked at the handcart, which Brom had run out under the arch. It was a low thing on two wheels, with a rail, and it smelled of hay. "I had not expected to go up to that building as matter. I find that I mind it less than I ought."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:63:Vastin put out one raw hand, when the boxes were going onto the cart, and touched Cael's sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:69:"I shall want to very much," said Vastin. "I have learned what it costs when I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:107:"You did," said Vastin, to the cobbles. It was a civil answer, and no more than any porter would have given; and Cael saw him hear how easily it had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:167:"I should not care to be thought to evade a beam," Vastin said. He said it slowly, and he said nothing after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:209:"It is," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:217:Vastin looked down the first flight for a moment, as he had once looked into a cut. Then he gave Cael the stick to carry, and turned about, and lowered himself by his hands until he sat upon the top step with his back to the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:287:"He has turned it over since she said *joins*," said Vastin. "I should know the look. It is a man deciding whether to show a subordinate how the thing is really done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:17:"I am told that it is the last time," said Vastin, to the cobbles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:71:Cleon had not sat down. He had fetched a sheet of his own from under the desk's slope, a square of the stiff stock that his cards were cut from, and a pen; and Cael saw, with some surprise, that he meant to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:85:The fifty-two sheets had lain there for eleven days in three heaps, as they had lain upon three floors. They were to go up at the bell, and a clerk was to write that they were gone. Lira lifted them one at a time, flat on her two forearms, the slow arm and the quick, while Brom held each box's lid. On the third box, six strides from the desk, sat Vastin, with his bound leg along a plank. He had the tarred case for a desk, and a leaf of Karis's notebook upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:93:"The Fallow's, eleven," said Lira now, from the first table. "That's the last of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:"Eleven," said Vastin, and made his stroke. "I have eleven. The box may be shut." He turned his head a little, toward the big man with the lid. "I should be obliged if it were said back to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:115:"It is sewn across the heel. I do not write with the heel." She had the brass strip from Ternhall in her right hand now, and she laid it along the line, to keep her eye from wandering into the one above. "I have two hands, and one line that wants both of them. I shall be slow. I should be obliged if nobody watched."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:121:"There's the writer's," said Cleon. The reed was in his fingers, an inch above the leaf. "And there's the knife's, coming through from the other side. They cross. You'll want to tell which is which before you call any of it a letter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:135:"The first run I can read," said Karis. She was writing with the bound hand, very small, and her lips were white. "It is the people at a kindling, as the face has them. The second run is six signs, and it is in the thin; and of the six I am certain of three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:145:"Your hand's better than mine on the fourth line," said Cleon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:155:"Good," said Cleon. "Then I'll not have to forget it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:The bell went with Cleon's finger on the last line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:181:Vastin had asked for his quarter of an hour again, and had been given it. They heard him above them on the second flight when they came to the stair's foot with the boxes: two hands set down, and a breath, and a scrape. He was at the head when they reached it, sitting against the iron with the case in his arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:241:"I shall walk for as long as I am able," said Vastin. "I shall say when I am not. I undertake that much, and I do not undertake a yard more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:*Two hundred and ninety-fourth day. Treswick; the loft, and one in the stall; the last night of both. A hundred and thirty. The back of the leaf: a list, nine lines, a thing and its number to each. Two copies, hers and the reader's, and his goes with her. The fourth line is the mark's; of its last six signs, three are sure. Fifty-two sheets counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote* withdrawn*. Vastin: fifteen of thirty, and the stair alone. Karis wrote with the sewn hand. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:"You have a twelfth," he said. "And you have told a registered practitioner of the Compact, beneath its oldest registry, how you came by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:Vastin did not speak; he looked at the two of them, and at the door, and then one raw hand came out from under the blanket and shut, slowly, into a fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:123:Shadow had come up at dusk, as it came every dusk, and he had told it no in a lane while his eyes ran. It had not lain down; it was full night now, in a town of doorways, and he was going to follow a man through it. There was a seal on that thing, and a reason for the seal which had not altered since Ostrand; and he had never felt it lean so against a shut door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:227:*No*, he said. He had said it to that one twice a day since Ostrand. He said it now as a person leans on a door with somebody leaning on the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:37:"The four went by the lane," he said, "two east and two west, at a walk, and they had their hoods back before the first corner. I did not see a face. The two below went out by the carts' gate, which had been left upon the latch for them." He stopped a step above Vastin, and looked at him for some time. "I could take one of the four, and I took the one who was there to be believed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:71:They had laid Vastin on the pallet under the blanketed window. He had not spoken since the cut, and Cael had thought him asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:129:Vastin could not hold a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:143:"I was told what it would cost," said Vastin. "She was exact, and I should like it remembered that she was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:155:*Iron-Wall-adjacent. Vastin. Partial. Iron family. Held. A plane, not a body: the ground told not to give. Distinct from the third — Iron-adjacent, the bed; this is not the bed hardened, this is a line drawn on it and kept. Engagement field: defensive; rooted. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Eleventh.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:169:He had it, then. It sat under his breastbone with the rest, as real as any of them, and as quiet as a gatepost in a field at night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:179:"I should be obliged for a word," said Vastin. "I regret that I cannot come to you for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:185:"I have been setting it in order," said Vastin. He spoke to the slates, and his voice had very little breath behind it. "It is a habit, and I am told that it is the tell; I shall give it up when I am stronger. There are three heads."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:223:He believed that Vastin would have done nothing with it. That was not the question, since it was not his to spend upon a belief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:283:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:293:*Eleventh. Iron Wall, from Vastin, in an alley, while he held a line for us with nothing behind him but the decision to. It's not Brom's — Brom gets harder, this holds. Since my Kindling I've learned to give ground and disappear. Now I've got the opposite in me, from the man who spent forty years being the wall they put in front of people like me. He held it for us instead. I don't have a word for what that is. I wrote it down anyway.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:55:"I have a copper," said Vastin. "I have had it ready since last night, in the expectation of being asked what I should cost to carry. I should prefer to pay it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:59:"You will not," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:109:It came to him quite plainly, between one stride and the next. A healer had made him write a thing in a book at a fork-stone: that inside Treswick he did not go near the station. He had kept it for thirteen days, to the letter, down a lane with four white alleys in it, and through a night when a thread of light on a loft's boards had not been steady. And the gate was behind him. The river was the Line, and he was over the river. The promise had been for inside, and he was out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:131:He had not thought of that when the copper was paid. Vastin sat on the board's edge with the bound leg laid along a coil and the tarred case across his thighs, and both raw hands upon the stick's head; and since he faced the way that they had come, he faced Cael. There was two yards of road between them. It did not grow or shrink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:139:"I have ridden up to that gate on four occasions," said Vastin. He spoke low, though the ground allowed more. "Each time I was met at the bridge. I certified what I was shown, and I rode down again, and I did not once see it go small behind me. One does not, from a saddle. One is looking at the next district."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:149:"You are thinking that I am owed something," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:179:It was Seln who put it on. He had been thirty yards ahead of the cobs all the morning. Where the last laid hedge gave out, and the ruts ran up onto open ground, he stood in the road with his right hand flat at his side, palm down, until the whole file had seen it. Nobody needed the words. The lean man spat out his straw. The girl who had talked for three miles about a broker's face shut her mouth on the rest, and every sigil on that road went into a pocket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:185:Vastin got down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:223:Nobody had spoken since the hedge. He had forgotten in thirteen days how loud that silence was. A town's noise is a blanket; a road's quiet is a room with every door open, and a person who carries twelve things that would each like to answer has the minding of all the doors. The quietest of them would lift at dusk. He could feel where it lay, as a person feels where the cold place is in a bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:293:The light was gone before he had ruled the line. Shadow lifted in him, as it lifted at every dusk, and the other eleven dropped away under it like a yard going quiet under snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:29:There was a fourth in that end of the gallery. Vastin sat on Brom's box by the nearest bay, six strides from the desk, with the bound leg out along its plank and both hands on the ash; he could not have followed a ladder, and he was only where he had been set down, as he was every morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:73:He had watched that woman read since a reading room above Ostrand. She read as some people eat, quickly, with her whole attention, and a page was gone before another person had found its first line. She did not read so now; her eyes went along one sign at a time, and her lips were shut. Twice the pencil went to the notebook and wrote a single letter, and went back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:109:"*In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong*" — her breath had given out upon a comma, and she took another without lifting her finger — "*there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:119:"*— set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it. And this is the reason of the apparatus, and this is all of its reason, and let none who come after forget it, though the un-bound be forgotten: that they should not come again.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:147:"That word stands here. It is spelled as he has spelled it, letter for letter, in the old signs. *The Architect.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:159:"The rest is what you heard. It says what the apparatus is for, and it says that there is no other reason. It was set over the land so that a kind of people should not come again." Her voice had dropped. "It calls them the un-bound. I said that word at this table on the first day, as a limit; I had only ever met it as the name of a layer in the ground. I did not know that it had been a people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:211:Cleon had opened his eyes to see where the slip lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:217:It was the stick that broke the quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:221:"The apparatus," he said. "The measure, the seat, the tier, the mark. He made it and he set it over the land. And the mark that is given at the kindling — "
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:239:"I was drafting," said Vastin. His voice had very little breath behind it. "I was told at that same spring, by another person, what it is when I do so. I was drawing a conclusion, in a room where I have been at some pains for nine days to draw nothing." He looked down at the ash. "I leave it where it stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:257:She looked at the pale place on the skin for some time. Then she took up the brass strip from Ternhall, which she had not had out of its cloth since the hide was rolled, and ruled a small box in the line that she was writing. It was four letters wide, by her own letters; she ruled it on all four sides, and left it empty, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:259:*and the* — there the box stood, with nothing in it — *set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:85:His left hand had come out of its sleeve, and it went to the strap that crossed the breast of his coat, and rested there, in the firelight, in front of six people. Cael had seen that hand go to that strap perhaps four times in all the roads since Ostrand; he had never seen it go there when anybody was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:125:"I held that for the whole of my service, and was easy in it. Above Ostrand I was shown that the fault was meant. I called it corrupt; I put down a seal upon that word, and walked three hundred miles on it, and thought that I had come to the floor." The stick's foot turned upon the cobbles. "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:185:"Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," he said. "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:213:"Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:243:Cleon said, "Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:263:They put Vastin into his stall; he went on the stick, and one shoulder, and let himself down into the straw by his hands. When he was down he lay looking at the boards over his head, where six people would sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:277:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:9:It was the factor. There was the leather apron, and the slate on its cord, and the pair of pale eyes that had gone along six people and a healer at a gate and counted them. They counted now. They went from a quiet man, who had come up level with the cobs, to four with loads; and they rested last upon the tail-board, where a pair of raw hands lay on an ash stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:45:"I had hoped that he would," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:65:Vastin had got down at the head, as he had undertaken to do for every slope. He was coming down the dry edge last of all, a tread at a time, with the bound leg swung out stiff; and forty strides from the top he was standing still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:71:"Here," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:75:Vastin looked at the wet stones for the space of several breaths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:91:"Two words," Vastin said. "Both of them ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:147:Vastin sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:183:Vastin had not risen. He could not have, in the time. He had let his shoulders come forward, and his head hang, and both hands go loose on the ash across his knees. He looked like an old carter who has nodded off on a stone after his supper. There was nothing upright in him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:223:Vastin got down off the tail-board for the last time, and paid his third copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:237:"Treswick. The walk under the town wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Cael stopped in the door. It was a little past sunrise on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, and the one small window laid a square of thin light across the whole of it. The courier's flat stood open at the table's head, with its six rows out and a pebble on each. The calfskin lay unrolled beneath them, under the Ternhall brass, and a dead man's book was open at its first page. Three of the Stair's sheets had been taken from their box and set in a row; and in the middle of everything, side by side, lay a leaf of her notebook and a square of stiff card with a turn of brown tape beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:He came round. Lira had the door, and Brom filled it a moment after, with a quiet man at his elbow who had not been seen to cross the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:27:Vastin had come to the door by then, on the stick, with the tarred case under his arm; he had stopped on the sill as a man stops who is not sure of a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:35:Vastin brought it to the table himself, and undid the strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:77:"It is three marks. Call them what you like." Karis took the pencil up. "But a grade grades something. The one that is given at a kindling grades a person, and there is a man in this room who signed for a good many. This one is not for size, as I showed you at the spring; a place of twice the measure has the same bar. It is not for which is fenced. So I have gone through everything else in which one of these sheets differs from another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:85:"It is a series number," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Thank you. That is exactly as much as I wanted." Her finger had gone to the hide, and it touched each ring in turn, going east. "The Fallow's newest sheet stands in its sixty-first year, and the Stair's in its ninety-fourth; the hall's is a hundred and thirty-eight. And the place behind the iron—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:"A series that was opened before the present manner of numbering is carried under its old style," said Vastin. "I should expect it, and I do not know when the manner changed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:151:It stood on the bare deal, a hand's breadth beyond the calfskin's edge, hard against the Ternhall strip; and the low sun from the window laid the brass's shadow over it, so that the point stood in a narrow dark line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:173:"Noted," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:223:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:259:The bench along the mill's wall was empty, at the end of a day. Vastin had come down the notch last, a tread at a time, behind the locked wheel; he reached the bench as the others reached the fence, and looked at it, and plainly thought it wiser to be found on his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:279:"Nineteen days," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:359:Lira looked at it there, and shut the hand, and opened it. She did not say anything; but she drew her sleeve down over the eight marks herself, which Cael had not seen her do since the Fallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:387:"I was told to stand there," said Vastin. "I was not told that I might leave off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:401:"Noted," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:27:The clerk wrote *the same*, and *four*. Vastin went down twenty-seven steps on his seat, with his heel in Cael's palm, and said at the foot that he believed he had improved upon his time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:97:He read it twice over, and the yard was very quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:189:Vastin had been looking at the two covered cups.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:199:"I see that it is," said Vastin. He turned his own cup a quarter round upon the board, and looked into it. "I was never shown the book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:217:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:223:*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:225:*One anomaly, as before. The still place. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Not touched since the waypost, and not to be until a healer has been asked.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:257:He looked at the three strides. Vastin slept in a stall now. The pallet by the gable had been rolled, and there was nothing between his knee and the blanket but boards. He could cross them. He could lift one corner of a horse-blanket, and see for himself whether the thing on the crown burned as it had always burned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:15:"A record," Cleon said, "sits."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:105:He had come to that town with a place under his breastbone which five readings had gone round and none had entered, and he had written at a fire that he wanted an instrument for what does not move. He had one now, resting an inch behind his eyes, as quiet as a folded rule. It would take no time; he need not so much as shift his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:129:Vastin had gone to the stair a quarter of an hour before the bell, by himself.
